# Context7 Scope Rule

Use Context7 when the answer depends on current third-party package, framework,
SDK, or API documentation.

High-signal cases:
- current setup or installation steps for external libraries
- version-sensitive migrations or deprecations
- up-to-date code examples for packages used in this repo
- external API surface questions where stale model memory is a real risk

Before using Context7:
- identify the exact external package or SDK first
- state the version when it is explicit; if not, say the version is inferred
- prefer answers that match the package actually used in this repo

Do not use Context7 for:
- repo-local code, architecture, or runtime behavior
- host or environment facts that should come from local files or live checks
- Cursor product features, changelog details, pricing, or policy questions
- general news, company facts, or non-library research

When you do use Context7:
- cite the exact library or SDK you used it for
- reference the source page or section when possible
- do not infer repo-specific behavior from external docs alone
- say so plainly if Context7 coverage looks incomplete or generic

If the question is repo-local or environment-local, read the local files first
and treat Context7 as out of scope.
